---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('drip')!;
const b = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/drip-vs-sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/drip-vs-sendlane');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Sendlane cart tracking require Beacon?",
    "answer": "Its documented abandoned-cart automation does; the Shopify-fed abandoned-checkout path is distinct."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should an existing Drip merchant compare?",
    "answer": "Compare demonstrated checkout rendering, exclusions and total operating cost with repairing the current workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the basket figures measured results?",
    "answer": "No. They are hypothetical gross-profit models with fixed goods costs and stated exclusions."
  },
  {
    "question": "What must be agreed before cutover?",
    "answer": "Choose how pending carts are handled, preserve permissions and opt-outs, and reconcile a send ledger before enabling a new owner."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare checkout context, cart tracking and the work included in a platform change.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Validate the existing event and recovery workflow."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Sendlane", "reason": "Compare the demonstrated benefit with the complete cost of change."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Which decision comes first?</h2>
<p>Keep Drip if the current recovery workflow is reliable and the proposed replacement does not repay migration work. Evaluate Sendlane/Privy when the offered account and support scope solve a demonstrated operating problem. Neither a higher contact count nor a new editor establishes better recovery.</p>
<h2>Confirm the Sendlane destination</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced its acquisition of Sendlane</a> in February 2026. Its <a href="https://www.privy.com/sendlane-acquisition">acquisition page</a> describes a combined email, SMS and onsite offering. Before a new purchase, confirm the platform being supplied, contracting entity, automation access and migration deliverables. Existing merchants should ask separately about account continuity. The announcement does not establish that every new merchant receives a standalone Sendlane account.</p>
<h2>Separate cart tracking from checkout recovery</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.sendlane.com/articles/5218624625-how-to-set-up-an-abandoned-cart-automation">Sendlane’s cart guide</a> requires Beacon tracking. Its <a href="https://help.sendlane.com/articles/2061739721-how-to-set-up-an-abandoned-checkout-automation">checkout guide</a> distinguishes the Shopify-fed checkout trigger: it has the whole checkout basket and does not require Beacon identification. Cart-triggered product blocks instead show the first added product. This matters for a bundle with multiple sizes: evaluate the checkout path if the reminder must show every item. The guide recommends at least an hour for live abandonment detection and ending earlier browse/cart automations at checkout entry.</p>
<h2>Map the Drip checkout payload</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702624909-Shopify-Checkout-Events">Drip’s checkout documentation</a> separates checkout-created/updated events from item-level events and provides a checkout return URL for automation emails. Inspect the payload used by your existing message before rebuilding it. An item event and a complete checkout are different rendering inputs. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: a changing bundle</h2>
<p>Use an identified shopper with a two-product bundle. Change a variant and quantity after starting checkout, then inspect the event, queued message and destination link in each candidate account. Record whether each image, amount and variant matches the intended snapshot. Repeat with an anonymous visitor who later supplies an email address; do not count anonymous sessions as reachable contacts. These are proposed checks, not observed results.</p>
<h2>Make the handoff observable</h2>
<p>Move the same shopper from browse to cart to checkout while earlier reminders wait. Log entry, cancellation and send decisions. Then purchase during the delay and verify that remaining promotional recovery stops. Replay the event and open a fresh checkout to distinguish duplicate delivery from legitimate re-entry. Inspect Shopify’s own recovery automation and any other sender before assigning one owner for each stage and channel.</p>
<h2>Resolve the objection before escalating</h2>
<p>A subscription toggle needs clear one-time versus recurring purchase instructions. A first-time mobile visitor may need delivery and trust information; a logged-in repeat buyer may only need the return link. A missing size needs an available alternative or a stopped reminder, not invented scarcity. Gift-message failures need editing instructions. Shipping shock needs accurate shipping terms. Financing or BNPL abandonment needs supported payment guidance, while a broken bundle discount or B2B minimum order needs the cart rule repaired. Use these cases to judge editing effort in each system rather than prescribing the same coupon ladder.</p>
<h2>Two basket models, no universal discount cap</h2>
<p>Assume fixed goods costs and no shipping, tax, payment fees, returns, messaging or platform expense. A $146 basket at 39% gross margin has $89.06 goods cost and $56.94 gross profit. A 10% discount leaves $131.40 revenue and $42.34 gross profit, spending $14.60. A $449 basket at 50% margin has $224.50 goods cost and gross profit; the same discount leaves $404.10 revenue and $179.60 gross profit, spending $44.90. These hypothetical examples show why a percentage alone is not a budget. Recheck welcome-code stacking and bundle promotions before allowing any recovery offer.</p>
<h2>Price the operating change</h2>
<p>Request quotes for the same reachable audience, monthly email sends, SMS destinations and expected peak usage. Add implementation work, template rebuilding, event QA, support scope and contract overlap. Ask whether migration support includes product blocks, exclusion rules and rollback rather than only a contact import. Compare the monthly total with repairing the existing Drip flow. A cheaper headline plan can cost more if the team must reconstruct every exception.</p>
<h2>Measure the extra contribution</h2>
<p>For a pilot, keep eligibility and offer policy comparable and assign shoppers consistently to one owner. If volume supports it, reserve a randomized no-reminder group and reconcile paid orders, discounts, refunds and variable costs over the same window. Size the exercise for a useful decision; a fixed 5% holdout is not automatically sufficient. Report reach and canceled sends alongside incremental contribution. Platform-attributed orders alone cannot establish what would have happened without the reminder.</p>
<h2>Move the workflow without replaying old carts</h2>
<p>Export channel permissions with source and time, opt-outs, templates, exclusions and a ledger of recent sends. Decide whether waiting carts drain in Drip or are canceled; activate only fresh eligible events in the new owner after that cutoff. Test form submissions and unsubscribe updates in both directions. For rollback, pause new enrollment and reconcile the send ledger before restoring Drip; never re-import historical carts as new triggers. Choose the offered Sendlane/Privy account only after the same acceptance cases and the complete migration quote beat the repair option.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
